<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1914 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Pōkarekare Ana</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Māori love song from about the time of the First World War, sung all over New Zealand and as far away as Korea.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional (probably communal; author not known)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (probably communal; author not known)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Māori of New Zealand</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Māori</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>north of Auckland, New Zealand</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>probably about 1914; published 1919</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional, probably communally composed c. 1914; first published 1919 by Paraire Tomoana and Āpirana Ngata, who did not claim it)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pokarekare-ana">Pōkarekare Ana</a>&quot; is a traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> love song in the Māori language, probably composed communally about the time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began in 1914. Its author is not known. The Eastern Māori politicians Paraire Tomoana and Āpirana Ngata published it in 1919, but neither claimed to have written it: they explained that it had come from north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> and had been made popular by Māori soldiers training near Auckland before they sailed for the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>The Māori words have hardly changed since, except that the waters named in the first line are made local. Some versions name Lake Rotorua in the North Island, which ties the song to the story of Hinemoa, who swam across the lake to her forbidden lover Tūtānekai on Mokoia Island. There have been many different English translations. The song was first written mostly in triple time, with the verse in duple time, but since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it has more often been heard in duple time.</p><p>It became one of the most popular songs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and has been used in advertising and by sporting groups: in &quot;Sailing Away&quot; for the country's 1987 America's Cup challenge, in Air New Zealand advertisements of the 1990s and 2000 (with a recording by Kiri Te Kanawa) and again in 2020, sung by Hayley Westenra, for the airline's 80th birthday. In April 2013 members and spectators in the New Zealand Parliament sang it after the house passed the bill legalising same-sex marriage. Dozens of singers have recorded it, among them the opera singers Kiri Te Kanawa and Malvina Major.</p><p>The song travelled far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. It is said to have reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> with New Zealand soldiers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, though there is no record of their spreading it; given Korean words and the title &quot;Yeonga&quot;, it became popular across the country. Since the 1960s it has been sung in Timor-Leste as &quot;Ha'u Hakerek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> Ida&quot;, with words by Momô dos Mártires, and its melody carries an Irish hymn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A love song of longing and separation, addressed to a loved one far away. The waters named in it change with the singers, so each community can make the song its own.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Māori</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Māori</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Pōkarekare ana,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They are stirred up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">ngā wai o Waiapu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the waters of Waiapu</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Whiti atu koe hine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When you cross over, girl,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">marino ana e.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they will be calm.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">E hine e,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O girl,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">hoki mai ra.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>come back here.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Ka mate ahau</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will die</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">I te aroha e.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of love.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Tuhituhi taku reta,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I write my letter,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">tuku atu taku rīngi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I send my ring,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Kia kite tō iwi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So that your people see</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">raru raru ana e.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>how troubled, troubled [I am].</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Whati whati taku pene</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My pen breaks and breaks</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">ka pau aku pepa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my paper is used up</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Ko taku aroha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But my love</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">mau tonu ana e.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>still holds fast.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">E kore te aroha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Love will never</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">e maroke i te rā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dry up in the sun</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Mākūkū tonu i</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It stays wet always with</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">aku roimata e.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my tears.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional Māori song; the text of Two Maori Songs (1926), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P%C5%8Dkarekare_Ana" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'Pōkarekare' is to be agitated, rough, choppy (of water); used of the troubled heart as well.</li><li>line 1.2: Waiapu is a river on the East Coast of the North Island; kept as a name.</li><li>line 1.3-4: 'Whiti atu koe' is literally 'you cross over'; the sense, that the water will be calm when she crosses, is implied by the following 'marino ana'. 'e' at the ends of lines is a vocative or sung ending, not translated.</li><li>line 3.2: 'rīngi' is a loanword from English 'ring'; 'reta' (3.1), 'pene' (4.1) and 'pepa' (4.2) are likewise from 'letter', 'pen', 'paper'.</li><li>line 3.4: 'raru raru ana' is 'troubled, in difficulty'; whose trouble it is (the singer's or the people's) is not spelt out; 'I am' is added.</li><li>line 4.1: 'Whati whati' is breaking repeatedly (or broken to pieces); rendered 'breaks and breaks'.</li><li>line 5.3-4: the source breaks the line after 'i' ('wet with / my tears'); the line division is kept.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="aQqn129MNYI" data-title="Pōkarekare Ana: Hayley Westenra (official channel)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Hayley Westenra (official channel)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Hayley Westenra - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aQqn129MNYI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sailing Away (&quot;All of Us&quot;)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1987</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted for New Zealand's America's Cup challenge</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Yeonga (Korean version)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new Korean words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ha'u Hakerek Surat Ida (Momô dos Mártires)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words in Timor-Leste</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Mhuire Mháthair, sé seo mo ghuí (Irish hymn)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Homophonous Hebrew translation (Ghil'ad Zuckermann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2007</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation keeping the sounds</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Crying Fist (South Korean film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2005</span><span class="sg-chain-h">theme song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Māori civilisation theme in Civilization VI: Gathering Storm (Geoff Knorr and Phill Boucher)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2019</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in, with &quot;Ka Mate&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Memories (Richard Clayderman)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1984</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pure (Hayley Westenra)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2003</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded on</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Soria Moria (Sissel)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1989</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded on</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hollie Steel charity single for the Christchurch earthquake</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Air New Zealand advertisements</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990s, 2000, 2020</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Story of Hinemoa and Tūtānekai</span><span class="sg-chain-h">versions naming Lake Rotorua are associated with it</span></li></ul></section>
